======================================================================================================
S3. THE as-MG ROW IS CLEAN -- mu and nu are RECIPROCAL, so mu takes the OBSERVED argument
======================================================================================================
  The flagged worry was that Amendment 3's as-MG row, built from mu(a_ex/a0), might be feeding
  an interpolation function the OBSERVED argument where it should take the NEWTONIAN one. It
  is not, and the reason is an identity rather than a numerical coincidence:
  [OK  ] alpha=1: nu(y) * mu(x) = 1 identically -- mu and nu are exact reciprocals of one another across the closure, so a prescription written as mu(observed) is NOT nu fed the wrong argument. It is a different, legitimate object
  [OK  ] alpha=2: nu(y) * mu(x) = 1 identically -- mu and nu are exact reciprocals of one another across the closure, so a prescription written as mu(observed) is NOT nu fed the wrong argument. It is a different, legitimate object

  CONSEQUENCE: the as-MG row carries NO argument error and NO correction is owed to it.
  Amendment 4's flag that it 'may not be assumed sound' is hereby discharged as a CLEAN BILL
  OF HEALTH -- which is worth exactly as much as finding a bug would have been, and is
  reported with the same emphasis it would have received had the bug been there.
  [OK  ] MUTATION: nu evaluated at the OBSERVED x is a genuinely different function from nu at the Newtonian y (difference nonzero), so the reciprocity above is a real exoneration and not a statement that argument choice never matters -- it matters, just not for mu

